      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No, it is not universally accepted; many historians and economists argue that labor unions often stifled productivity, suppressed wages for non-union workers, and created inefficiencies that slowed down the American economy. While unions certainly protected their own members, they frequently acted as a barrier to innovation and fair competition, leading to higher prices for consumers and reduced economic growth. The idea that they were the sole or primary driver of middle-class stability is a one-sided narrative that ignores the significant economic costs and negative externalities they imposed on the broader market.",
